export const HOME_FAQS = [
  { q: "What insurance does a spray foam contractor need?", a: "A spray foam contractor typically needs general liability, off-ratio coverage (either as an endorsement or separate policy), contractor pollution liability for isocyanate and off-gassing exposure, workers' compensation, commercial auto for your rigs and trucks, tools and equipment coverage for your proportioners and hose sets, and a commercial umbrella above it all. Most operations also need contractor bonds for licensing in certain states. I bundle these into one coordinated program." },
  { q: "What is off-ratio coverage and why do I need it?", a: "Off-ratio coverage addresses claims arising from spray foam that was applied at an incorrect chemical ratio — meaning the A-side and B-side components weren't balanced properly. Off-ratio foam can fail to cure correctly, lose adhesion, cause moisture intrusion, or off-gas at elevated levels. Standard contractor GL policies typically exclude these claims explicitly. Off-ratio coverage is the endorsement or separate policy that fills that gap — and it's something most generalist agents don't even know to ask about." },
  { q: "Does standard GL cover spray foam contractors?", a: "Standard GL covers basic third-party bodily injury and property damage, but most standard contractor GL policies contain exclusions that hit spray foam hard — off-ratio foam exclusions, pollution exclusions (isocyanates and blowing agents), and completed-operations carve-outs for chemical applications. A standard GL might technically 'cover' your operation while excluding the claims that are most likely to hit you." },
  { q: "Are isocyanates covered under my general liability policy?", a: "Almost certainly not without a specific pollution endorsement or contractor pollution liability policy. Courts in multiple jurisdictions have ruled that MDI, TDI, and other isocyanates qualify as 'pollutants' under the standard GL pollution exclusion. That means a claim alleging your foam off-gassing caused respiratory issues or property damage gets denied under your GL. I add contractor pollution liability to every spray foam program I write." },
  { q: "How much does spray foam contractor insurance cost?", a: "It depends on your revenue, crew size, rig count, whether you do open-cell or closed-cell, commercial versus residential work, your loss history, and which states you work in. Owner-operators can expect to pay a few thousand a year for a basic GL program; larger multi-rig commercial operations with off-ratio and pollution coverage will pay more. I quote your actual operation in 15 minutes — not a generic estimate." },
  { q: "What is contractor pollution liability and does a spray foam contractor need it?", a: "Contractor pollution liability (CPL) covers bodily injury and property damage claims arising from chemical exposures your work creates — including isocyanate off-gassing, blowing agent releases, and solvent exposure during foam removal. Standard GL excludes these under the pollution exclusion. If you're spraying SPF, you almost certainly need CPL. I include it in every spray foam program I put together." },
  { q: "What class codes apply to spray foam workers?", a: "Spray foam workers are often misclassified under painting codes (9400-series) or generic insulation codes. The correct classification depends on whether your crews are doing residential or commercial foam, interior or exterior, open-cell or closed-cell — each can carry different codes. Getting this right matters: wrong codes mean you overpay at audit or have coverage gaps when a chemical exposure claim hits. I assign class codes to your actual workflow." },
  { q: "Does workers' comp cover chemical exposure injuries for spray foam crews?", a: "Yes — properly structured workers' comp covers medical treatment, disability benefits, and lost wages for injuries arising from your crews' work, including chemical exposure from isocyanates, blowing agents, and solvents. The key is making sure your policy is rated for the actual exposure of spray foam work — not a misclassified generic insulation code that underfunds the risk." },
  { q: "Is my spray rig covered under a standard contractor policy?", a: "Typically no. Standard contractor GL and even most business auto policies exclude tools and equipment — especially specialty equipment like proportioners, reactors, hose sets, and heated trailers. Your spray rig needs inland marine (tools and equipment) coverage scheduled at its actual replacement value. I make sure this is part of every spray foam program I place." },
  { q: "What does tools and equipment coverage cover for spray foam contractors?", a: "Tools and equipment (inland marine) coverage covers your proportioners, reactors, spray guns, hose sets, heated trailers, and other spray equipment against theft, damage, and loss. I schedule spray rigs at their actual replacement cost — not a depreciated value that won't cover what it costs to replace a Graco E-30 or Reactor 2 today." },
  { q: "What is a completed-operations claim in spray foam contracting?", a: "A completed-operations claim arises after you've finished a job and left the site. A common example: spray foam you applied six months ago is now alleged to have failed — causing moisture intrusion, structural damage, or health issues from off-gassing. Standard GL includes completed-operations coverage, but many policies exclude spray foam specifically under off-ratio or product exclusions. I make sure your GL responds to completed-operations claims, not just active job-site incidents." },
  { q: "Will my insurance cover foam removal if a job goes wrong?", a: "Coverage for foam removal depends on the cause and how your policy is written. If the foam failed due to an off-ratio application (the most common scenario), your policy needs specific off-ratio coverage to respond. If the failure is attributed to pollution or chemical exposure, contractor pollution liability needs to be in place. A standard GL policy that excludes off-ratio and pollution probably won't respond to a foam-removal claim." },
  { q: "Do I need a commercial umbrella as a spray foam contractor?", a: "Yes, in most cases. Completed-operations claims from spray foam — especially on commercial buildings — can produce judgments or settlements well above standard GL limits ($1M per occurrence, $2M aggregate). A commercial umbrella provides additional limits above your GL, auto, and workers' comp. I size the umbrella to your real exposure, not just recommend a default $1M that may not be enough." },
  { q: "Can you insure a spray foam contractor who has had prior claims?", a: "Often yes. I have admitted and E&S (excess and surplus lines) markets for spray foam contractors with prior completed-operations losses, off-ratio claims, or other loss runs that standard carriers won't touch. Bring me your loss history and I'll find the right market — I've placed hard spray foam risks that other agents gave up on." },
  { q: "Does commercial auto cover my spray rig and trailer?", a: "Your truck and trailer are covered under commercial auto for liability and physical damage when on public roads. The spray equipment inside the trailer or on the rig is a separate tools and equipment (inland marine) question — commercial auto doesn't cover the proportioner and hose set. I coordinate both so the rig and the equipment are both fully covered." },
  { q: "Do you write spray foam insurance in all 50 states?", a: "Yes. I'm licensed in all 50 states and have specialty markets for spray foam operations everywhere in the country — Texas, Florida, the Southeast, Midwest, Northeast, California, Pacific Northwest, and beyond." },
  { q: "How fast can I get a spray foam insurance quote?", a: "15 minutes on a call, usually. I know what questions to ask and which markets to go to, so I don't need a two-week back-and-forth to put together a spray foam program. For larger operations or complex loss histories I'll tell you upfront if I need more time." },
  { q: "What makes a spray foam contractor a hard risk to insure?", a: "Several things make spray foam a specialty risk: the off-ratio exclusion in standard GL, isocyanates qualifying as pollutants, high equipment values, chemical exposure workers' comp, and the long tail of completed-operations claims. Most generalist agents don't know how to navigate these issues. That's exactly why I exist — I solve these problems every day." },
  { q: "Should I bundle all my spray foam coverage into one program?", a: "Yes. Bundling GL, off-ratio coverage, pollution liability, workers' comp, tools and equipment, and commercial auto into one coordinated program closes gaps between policies and usually costs less than buying separate policies from separate carriers. It also means one agent — me — who understands how the pieces fit together when a claim hits." },
  { q: "What's the difference between open-cell and closed-cell foam for insurance purposes?", a: "Both require the same basic coverage types, but closed-cell commercial jobs typically carry higher equipment values, larger completed-operations limits, and more significant pollution exposure from the blowing agents used. Open-cell residential work has different class-code implications for workers' comp and typically lower per-job liability. I write programs for both — and for contractors who do both." },
];

